<section class="cp-section" id="track-record">
  <h2 class="cp-section-title">Broker Track Records and Regulator Records</h2>

  <h3>Legality and Regulatory Status in Pakistan</h3>
  <p>Pakistan's regulatory position on offshore forex and CFD platforms requires care. The State Bank of Pakistan has issued notices concerning payments for offshore forex, margin trading, and CFD platforms. Before funding an account, review the <a href="https://www.sbp.org.pk/EPD/2022/FECL8.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official SBP circular</a> and current rules. Do not assume that an offshore broker is authorised to operate in Pakistan.</p>

  <h3>Leverage Limits Depend on the Broker Entity</h3>
  <p>Retail leverage depends on the legal entity holding your account and the rules that apply to it. FCA-regulated retail CFD accounts generally have leverage limits of up to 1:30 on major currency pairs. Offshore entities may advertise substantially higher leverage, but this increases the risk of rapid losses. Confirm the exact terms for your account before trading.</p>

  <h3>Tax and Reporting Responsibilities</h3>
  <p>Tax treatment depends on the nature of the income, your circumstances, and applicable Pakistani tax rules. Do not assume that all forex profits or broker transfers receive the same tax treatment. Consult current <a href="https://www.fbr.gov.pk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBR guidance</a> or a qualified tax professional about reporting requirements and any tax due.</p>

  <h3>Client Protection and Compensation</h3>
  <p>Protections vary by legal entity and regulator. Some regulated entities may offer segregated client funds, negative balance protection, or access to a compensation scheme when eligibility conditions are met. These protections are not universal and do not guarantee reimbursement for trading losses. Check the regulator's rules and the exact entity named in your client agreement.</p>

  <h3>How to Verify a Broker's Record</h3>
  <p>Search the exact legal entity name and licence number in the relevant regulator's official register. Review warnings, enforcement notices, permitted activities, and the date of any published action. A record concerning one company does not automatically apply to every entity using the same broker brand.</p>

